"""Additional executed examples: strings, matrices, bit tricks, iterators, C/Java/C++ extras, runtime-error explanations."""
from gen21_algo_lib import *

X("python", "algo_select", "medium", "How do I rotate a matrix 90 degrees clockwise in Python?",
  'def rotate(m):\n    return [list(row) for row in zip(*m[::-1])]\n\nfor row in rotate([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]):\n    print(row)',
  "Reverse the order of the rows, then transpose with `zip(*...)`. The `*` unpacks the rows as separate arguments, and `zip` groups the i-th element of each. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\nThis builds a new matrix in O(n^2) time and space. Rotating in place needs a layer-by-layer swap of four cells at a time.")
X("python", "algo_select", "medium", "Find the k most frequent elements efficiently.",
  'import heapq\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef top_k(items, k):\n    counts = Counter(items)\n    return heapq.nlargest(k, counts, key=lambda x: (counts[x], -ord(x[0])))\n\nprint(top_k("a b a c b a d b a c".split(), 2))',
  "Count with a hash map (O(n)), then pick the top k with a heap (O(m log k) for m distinct items), which beats sorting all distinct items when k is small. Output: `{out}`. Counting sort by frequency (bucket by count) can reach O(n) overall. The tie-break in the key makes the result deterministic.")
X("python", "algo_select", "hard", "How do I check if a string is a valid permutation of another one efficiently?",
  'from collections import Counter\n\ndef is_permutation(a, b):\n    return len(a) == len(b) and Counter(a) == Counter(b)\n\ndef find_anagram_windows(text, pattern):\n    need, k = Counter(pattern), len(pattern)\n    window = Counter(text[:k])\n    hits = [0] if window == need else []\n    for i in range(k, len(text)):\n        window[text[i]] += 1\n        old = text[i - k]\n        window[old] -= 1\n        if window[old] == 0:\n            del window[old]\n        if window == need:\n            hits.append(i - k + 1)\n    return hits\n\nprint(is_permutation("listen", "silent"), find_anagram_windows("cbaebabacd", "abc"))',
  "Two strings are permutations of each other if their character counts match: O(n) instead of sorting both for O(n log n). The second function slides a window of the pattern's length across the text, updating counts incrementally (add the new character, remove the old) instead of recounting each window. Output: `{out}`. The `del` when a count hits zero matters, since `Counter` equality treats zero counts as different from missing keys in older Python versions.")
X("python", "algo_select", "medium", "Explain bit manipulation tricks: counting set bits and finding the single number.",
  'def popcount(n):\n    c = 0\n    while n:\n        n &= n - 1      # clears the lowest set bit\n        c += 1\n    return c\n\ndef single(nums):\n    r = 0\n    for x in nums:\n        r ^= x\n    return r\n\nprint(popcount(0b101101), single([4, 1, 2, 1, 2]), 13 & -13)',
  "`n & (n - 1)` clears the lowest set bit, so the loop runs once per set bit. XOR cancels equal pairs (`x ^ x == 0`, and `x ^ 0 == x`), so XOR-ing everything leaves the number that appears once, in O(n) time and O(1) space. `n & -n` isolates the lowest set bit. Output: `{out}` (four set bits, the unique value 4, and the lowest set bit of 13).", "4 4 1")
X("python", "algo_select", "medium", "How does Python's iterator protocol work? Show a custom iterator.",
  'class Countdown:\n    def __init__(self, start):\n        self.n = start\n    def __iter__(self):\n        return self\n    def __next__(self):\n        if self.n <= 0:\n            raise StopIteration\n        self.n -= 1\n        return self.n + 1\n\nprint(list(Countdown(4)), sum(Countdown(4)))',
  "A `for` loop calls `iter(obj)` to get an iterator, then calls `next()` on it repeatedly until `StopIteration` is raised. An object that implements `__iter__` and `__next__` follows the protocol, so it works with `for`, `list`, `sum` and unpacking. Output: `{out}`. A generator function is usually a shorter way to write the same thing.", "[4, 3, 2, 1] 10")
X("python", "algo_select", "hard", "Merge k sorted lists - which approach and why?",
  'import heapq\n\ndef merge_k(lists):\n    heap = [(l[0], i, 0) for i, l in enumerate(lists) if l]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    out = []\n    while heap:\n        val, i, j = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        out.append(val)\n        if j + 1 < len(lists[i]):\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (lists[i][j + 1], i, j + 1))\n    return out\n\nprint(merge_k([[1, 4, 7], [2, 5], [3, 6, 9], []]))\nprint(merge_k([[1, 4, 7], [2, 5], [3, 6, 9]]) == list(heapq.merge([1, 4, 7], [2, 5], [3, 6, 9])))',
  "Keep a min-heap holding the current front element of each list. Pop the smallest, then push the next element from that same list. The heap never holds more than k items, so each of the N output values costs O(log k): O(N log k) total, better than concatenating and sorting (O(N log N)) and better than merging pairwise repeatedly. Output:\n\n```\n{out}\n```\n\n`heapq.merge` does this lazily in the standard library.")
X("python", "ds_impl", "hard", "Implement an iterative in-order traversal of a binary tree.",
  'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, v, l=None, r=None):\n        self.v, self.l, self.r = v, l, r\n\ndef inorder(root):\n    out, stack, cur = [], [], root\n    while cur or stack:\n        while cur:\n            stack.append(cur)\n            cur = cur.l\n        cur = stack.pop()\n        out.append(cur.v)\n        cur = cur.r\n    return out\n\ntree = Node(4, Node(2, Node(1), Node(3)), Node(6, Node(5), Node(7)))\nprint(inorder(tree))',
  "Go as far left as possible while pushing nodes on a stack, pop and visit a node, then move to its right child and repeat. The explicit stack replaces the call stack, avoiding recursion-depth problems on very deep trees. Output: `{out}`: sorted order, because this is a search tree. It is O(n) time and O(h) space for tree height h.", "[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]")
X("python", "ds_impl", "medium", "Build a simple graph class with adjacency lists and show degree counts.",
  'from collections import defaultdict\n\nclass Graph:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.adj = defaultdict(set)\n    def add_edge(self, a, b):\n        self.adj[a].add(b)\n        self.adj[b].add(a)\n    def degree(self, n):\n        return len(self.adj[n])\n\ng = Graph()\nfor a, b in [("x", "y"), ("x", "z"), ("y", "z"), ("z", "w")]:\n    g.add_edge(a, b)\nprint({n: g.degree(n) for n in sorted(g.adj)})',
  "An adjacency list maps each node to its neighbours, using O(V + E) space. That's the right default for sparse graphs. An adjacency matrix uses O(V^2) space and gives O(1) edge lookups, which only pays off for dense graphs. Using sets prevents duplicate edges. Output: `{out}`.")
X("python", "optimise", "hard", "Is it faster to build a string with join or +=? Prove it without timing.",
  'import sys\n\nparts = [str(i) for i in range(1000)]\njoined = "".join(parts)\nconcat = ""\nfor p in parts:\n    concat += p\nprint(joined == concat, len(joined))',
  "Both produce the same string (`{out}`), but they differ in how much copying happens: `+=` may copy the growing string on every iteration (O(n^2) in the worst case), while `join` computes the total length once and copies each piece once (O(n)). CPython has an optimisation that sometimes makes `+=` fast, but other implementations don't, so rely on `join`. If you want proof, count allocations or measure with `timeit` on your machine.")
X("python", "optimise", "medium", "Does using a dict instead of if/elif chain help speed?",
  'def op_chain(name, a, b):\n    if name == "add": return a + b\n    elif name == "sub": return a - b\n    elif name == "mul": return a * b\n    elif name == "div": return a / b\n\nOPS = {"add": lambda a, b: a + b, "sub": lambda a, b: a - b, "mul": lambda a, b: a * b, "div": lambda a, b: a / b}\n\nprint(all(op_chain(n, 6, 3) == OPS[n](6, 3) for n in OPS), OPS["mul"](6, 3))',
  "For four branches the speed difference is negligible; the real benefit is structure: a dispatch table is data, so you can add operations without touching control flow, validate names with `in`, and test it easily. Both versions give the same results: `{out}`. Optimise for clarity first and measure before assuming a speed-up.")

# ---------- C ----------
X("c", "c_memory", "medium", "How do I read a line safely in C instead of using gets?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    char buf[16];\n    const char *input = "a rather long line of text\\n";\n    FILE *f = tmpfile();\n    if (!f) { puts("tmpfile failed"); return 1; }\n    fputs(input, f);\n    rewind(f);\n    if (fgets(buf, sizeof buf, f)) {\n        buf[strcspn(buf, "\\n")] = 0;\n        printf("[%s] %zu\\n", buf, strlen(buf));\n    }\n    fclose(f);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "`gets` was removed from the C standard because it cannot limit input length. `fgets(buf, size, stream)` reads at most `size - 1` characters and always NUL-terminates; it keeps the newline if it fits, so strip it with `strcspn`. The demo writes the text to a temporary file and reads it back, so it behaves like reading from a real stream. Output: `{out}` - `fgets` stopped after `sizeof buf - 1` characters, leaving the rest of the line unread.")
X("c", "c_struct", "medium", "How do I implement a linked list node insertion and traversal in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\ntypedef struct Node { int v; struct Node *next; } Node;\n\nNode *push_front(Node *head, int v) {\n    Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);\n    if (!n) exit(1);\n    n->v = v; n->next = head;\n    return n;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    Node *head = NULL;\n    for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) head = push_front(head, i);\n    for (Node *p = head; p; p = p->next) printf("%d ", p->v);\n    printf("\\n");\n    while (head) { Node *t = head->next; free(head); head = t; }\n    return 0;\n}',
  "Each node holds a value and a pointer to the next node. Pushing at the front is O(1): allocate, point the new node at the old head, and return it as the new head. Traversal follows `next` until `NULL`. When freeing, save `next` **before** freeing the node, or you'll read freed memory. Output: `{out}` (the last pushed item comes first).", "4 3 2 1")
X("c", "c_bits", "medium", "What does sizeof tell me about struct padding?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stddef.h>\n\nstruct A { char c; int i; char d; };\nstruct B { int i; char c; char d; };\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf("%d %d\\n", sizeof(struct A) > sizeof(struct B), offsetof(struct A, i) > 1);\n    return 0;\n}',
  "The compiler inserts padding so each member is aligned to its natural boundary, so the order of members can change the size. Putting the larger members first usually wastes less space. The exact sizes depend on the platform, so I print only the comparisons: `{out}` (struct A is bigger, and `i` is not at offset 1). Check with `sizeof` and `offsetof` when you care about memory layout or binary file formats.")
X("c", "c_strings", "medium", "How do I parse a string into numbers safely in C?",
  '#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    const char *tests[] = {"123", "12x", "", "99999999999999999999"};\n    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\n        char *end;\n        errno = 0;\n        long v = strtol(tests[i], &end, 10);\n        int ok = errno == 0 && end != tests[i] && *end == 0;\n        printf("%d ", ok);\n        if (ok) printf("(%ld) ", v);\n    }\n    printf("\\n");\n    return 0;\n}',
  "`atoi` can't report errors. `strtol` can: it sets `end` to the first unparsed character and sets `errno` on overflow. A parse is valid if something was consumed (`end != start`), nothing is left over (`*end == 0`) and `errno` is unchanged. Output: `{out}` - only \"123\" is valid.")

# ---------- C++ ----------
X("cpp", "cpp_stl", "medium", "How do I use std::unordered_map with a custom key type?",
  '#include <functional>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <unordered_map>\n\nstruct P { int x, y; bool operator==(const P &o) const { return x == o.x && y == o.y; } };\nstruct PHash { size_t operator()(const P &p) const { return std::hash<int>()(p.x) * 31 + std::hash<int>()(p.y); } };\n\nint main() {\n    std::unordered_map<P, const char *, PHash> m;\n    m[{1, 2}] = "a";\n    m[{3, 4}] = "b";\n    std::cout << m.at({1, 2}) << " " << m.count({9, 9}) << "\\n";\n}',
  "An unordered container needs two things for a custom key: an equality comparison and a hash function. Equal keys must produce equal hashes. Combine the field hashes with a multiplier or a proper mixing function to avoid many collisions. Output: `{out}`. For ordered containers (`std::map`), you only need `operator<`.")
X("cpp", "cpp_error", "medium", "What happens if I use an iterator after the vector reallocates?",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3};\n    v.reserve(3);\n    auto before = v.data();\n    v.push_back(4);          // exceeds capacity: storage is reallocated\n    std::cout << (before != v.data()) << "\\n";\n}',
  "When a vector grows beyond its capacity it allocates new storage and moves the elements, which **invalidates** all iterators, pointers and references to the old elements. Using them afterwards is undefined behaviour. Here the data pointer changed: `{out}`. Fix it by re-acquiring iterators after modifications, using indexes, or reserving enough capacity beforehand.")
X("cpp", "cpp_stl", "medium", "Show how std::optional or a pair can return 'maybe a value' from a search.",
  '#include <iostream>\n#include <map>\n#include <string>\n#include <utility>\n\nstd::pair<bool, int> lookup(const std::map<std::string, int> &m, const std::string &k) {\n    auto it = m.find(k);\n    if (it == m.end()) return {false, 0};\n    return {true, it->second};\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::map<std::string, int> m = {{"a", 1}, {"b", 2}};\n    auto r1 = lookup(m, "b"), r2 = lookup(m, "z");\n    std::cout << r1.first << ":" << r1.second << " " << r2.first << "\\n";\n}',
  "A search can fail, so the return type should say so. Never use a magic value like -1 when -1 could be legitimate data. A `pair<bool, T>` works in any C++ version; `std::optional<T>` (C++17) is clearer. Also note that `m[key]` on a `std::map` **inserts** a default value when the key is missing - use `find` or `count` for pure lookups. Output: `{out}`.")

# ---------- Java ----------
X("java", "java_collections", "medium", "How do I use a PriorityQueue with a comparator in Java?",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    record Job(String name, int priority) {}\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        PriorityQueue<Job> q = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Job::priority).reversed());\n        q.add(new Job("low", 1));\n        q.add(new Job("urgent", 9));\n        q.add(new Job("normal", 5));\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        while (!q.isEmpty()) sb.append(q.poll().name()).append(" ");\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n    }\n}',
  "`PriorityQueue` is a min-heap by natural order. Provide a `Comparator` to change the order; reversing it makes the highest priority come out first. `add` and `poll` are O(log n), `peek` is O(1). Iterating over the queue does not give sorted order, so always `poll`. Output: `{out}`.", "urgent normal low")
X("java", "java_basics", "medium", "Explain Java's pass-by-value with objects, with a runnable example.",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void mutate(List<Integer> xs) { xs.add(99); }\n    static void reassign(List<Integer> xs) { xs = new ArrayList<>(List.of(-1)); }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2));\n        mutate(a);\n        reassign(a);\n        System.out.println(a);\n    }\n}',
  "Java always passes by value, but for objects the value is a **reference**. The method can use the reference to change the object (`add` is visible to the caller), but reassigning the parameter only changes the method's local copy of the reference. Output: `{out}`.", "[1, 2, 99]")
X("java", "java_sorting", "hard", "Implement a binary heap in Java to understand how PriorityQueue works.",
  'import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class MinHeap {\n        private final List<Integer> a = new ArrayList<>();\n        void add(int x) {\n            a.add(x);\n            int i = a.size() - 1;\n            while (i > 0 && a.get((i - 1) / 2) > a.get(i)) {\n                Collections.swap(a, i, (i - 1) / 2);\n                i = (i - 1) / 2;\n            }\n        }\n        int poll() {\n            int top = a.get(0);\n            int last = a.remove(a.size() - 1);\n            if (!a.isEmpty()) {\n                a.set(0, last);\n                int i = 0;\n                while (true) {\n                    int l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;\n                    if (l < a.size() && a.get(l) < a.get(m)) m = l;\n                    if (r < a.size() && a.get(r) < a.get(m)) m = r;\n                    if (m == i) break;\n                    Collections.swap(a, i, m);\n                    i = m;\n                }\n            }\n            return top;\n        }\n        boolean isEmpty() { return a.isEmpty(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        MinHeap h = new MinHeap();\n        for (int x : new int[]{5, 3, 8, 1, 9, 2}) h.add(x);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        while (!h.isEmpty()) sb.append(h.poll()).append(" ");\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n    }\n}',
  "A binary heap stores a complete tree in an array: the children of index `i` are at `2i+1` and `2i+2`. `add` appends and sifts up while the parent is larger; `poll` takes the root, moves the last element to the root and sifts down. Both are O(log n). Repeatedly polling returns the values in sorted order (heap sort): `{out}`.", "1 2 3 5 8 9")
X("java", "java_concurrency", "medium", "What is the difference between synchronized and AtomicInteger?",
  'import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger();\n        Thread[] ts = new Thread[4];\n        for (int i = 0; i < ts.length; i++) {\n            ts[i] = new Thread(() -> { for (int j = 0; j < 10000; j++) counter.incrementAndGet(); });\n            ts[i].start();\n        }\n        for (Thread t : ts) t.join();\n        System.out.println(counter.get());\n    }\n}',
  "A plain `int` incremented from several threads loses updates because `count++` is read-modify-write. `synchronized` blocks make a section of code mutually exclusive; `AtomicInteger` performs the increment as a single atomic hardware-supported operation, which is cheaper for simple counters and cannot deadlock. The total is always `{out}`. Use locks when several variables must change together consistently.", "40000")
